// int_vector_tests.nv — the integer formatter against CPython.
//
// WHERE THE EXPECTED STRINGS COME FROM
//
// Every one of them was produced by CPython 3 — `str(v)` for signed
// decimal, `format(v & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, "b"/"o"/"x")` for the
// unsigned radices — and pasted in.  Nothing in this file was worked
// out by hand, which is the point: a hand-computed expectation is a
// second implementation of the thing under test, written by the same
// person, at the same time, from the same misunderstanding.
//
// The corpus is not random sampling with a nice name either.  It holds
// every boundary a digit-count can turn on: zero, plus and minus one,
// `Int.min` and `Int.max` and their neighbours, every power of ten,
// every power of two, and every power of eight and sixteen inside the
// range, each with the value below and above it.  Then 100 pseudo-random
// values on a fixed seed on top, because a boundary corpus tests the
// author's imagination and a random one does not.

use std.test
use digits
use numstr

@test
fn test_signed_dec_against_cpython_1() [io]
    let vals = [-9223372036854775808, -9223372036854775807,
